{%- comment -%}
  Returns fine print, self-enabling.

  Renders ONLY when a refund policy actually exists in Settings → Policies. Until
  then it outputs nothing, so the storefront can never promise a returns term the
  business has not published. The moment the policy goes live this appears on its
  own, with no theme edit.

  COMPLIANCE — why this sits on the product page and not only in the footer:
  material terms must be *clear and conspicuous*, which means visible at the point
  of decision. A restrictive term (used items are non-returnable) that a buyer
  only discovers after purchase is the classic deceptive-omission pattern, and it
  is also what turns a refund request into a chargeback. Stating it plainly next
  to the buy button protects the term rather than weakening it: a disclosed
  restriction is enforceable, a hidden one is not.

  Accepts:
  - context: {String} 'product' | 'cart' — controls how much detail is shown
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if shop.refund_policy != blank and shop.refund_policy.body != blank -%}
  <div class="sw-returns-note sw-returns-note--{{ context | default: 'product' }}">
    <p class="sw-returns-note__line">
      {%- render 'stridewell-icon', icon: 'return-arrow', class: 'sw-icon--sm' -%}
      <span>
        {%- if context == 'cart' -%}
          Unused items can be returned. See our
          <a href="{{ shop.refund_policy.url }}">{{ shop.refund_policy.title }}</a>.
        {%- else -%}
          <strong>Unused items only.</strong> For hygiene reasons we can't accept
          returns on items that have been worn or tried on. Damaged, faulty or
          incorrect items are always covered. Full terms in our
          <a href="{{ shop.refund_policy.url }}">{{ shop.refund_policy.title }}</a>.
        {%- endif -%}
      </span>
    </p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
